/-!
# Euler data to arithmetic Prony nodes

There are two distinct arithmetic node constructions.

First, a positive integer address `n` has lifted golden coordinate
`log n / goldenScalePeriod`. Sampling that coordinate with a complex Mellin
step `tau` produces the exact Euler character `n ^ (-tau)`. Therefore every
finite von Mangoldt Dirichlet window along an arithmetic progression in the
Mellin parameter is an exact finite Prony trace.

Second, the full von Mangoldt L-series equals `-zeta'/zeta` on `Re s > 1`.
The latter expression supplies its canonical meromorphic continuation. At a
stored nontrivial zero of multiplicity `m`, its punctured-neighborhood
principal part is `-m / (s-rho)`. Mapping the pole center by the common golden
exponential coordinate gives an actual zero-side Prony node. Functional
equation reflection sends that node to its inverse, and unit radius is exactly
the critical-line condition.

The zero-side exponential map is periodic in the vertical direction. Finite
Vandermonde recovery therefore carries an explicit node-injectivity hypothesis.
The module does not infer that hypothesis from known zeta-zero spacing, and it
does not prove RH.
-/

/- Library-search audit trail (2026-09-03):
   * `EulerProduct.single_address_heat_trace_eq_log_derivative` already owns the
     right-half-plane von Mangoldt identity.
   * `ZeroSum.HasZetaZeroMultiplicity` already owns the local analytic-unit
     factorization of zeta at every stored zero.
   * `FiniteCrystalTimeFrequencyBridge` already owns finite Prony/Vandermonde
     observability, so no second delay-coordinate API is introduced.
   * `GoldenSamplingAtom` already owns the radial golden normalization. The
     imported `GoldenExponentialPronyCoordinate` packages its complex character
     and time-power law.
   * No D5 declaration currently composes these owners into the finite Euler
     trace, the continued Euler principal part, and the actual zero-pole node.
   * Pinned Mathlib supplies complex powers, logarithmic derivatives, local
     congruence of logarithmic derivatives, and analytic nonvanishing near an
     analytic unit. -/

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

noncomputable section

